{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792252889,"nonce":"+YDXwOZDMoKFrmMMpbVQ3JyUQVo=","title":"Python: list, tuple and `in` of instances ignore __eq__","message":"A typed list or tuple of class instances compares its elements by identity, and `in` does the same, so a class with `__eq__` is never equal to another instance:\n\n    class Same(object):\n        def __eq__(self, other):\n            return isinstance(other, Same)\n    s = Same()\n    print([s] == [Same()], (s,) == (Same(),), [s] != [Same()], s in [Same()])\n\nCPython prints `True True False True`; zypy prints `False False True False`. A scalar `s == Same()` calls `__eq__` correctly. The element comparison of a list or tuple of `Class(k)` must call the class's `__eq__` when it defines one (or `zb_any_eq` on the boxes), and identity only when no class in the element's range overrides it.","files":null}]}